From: Jay Leaton <58495321+jayleaton@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 04:58:35 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 21/44] docs: the router's side-by-side outputs, and the
 measured cost that remains

Decode 33.19 tok/s through the served API against 123.7 for the plain control and
83.1 for 56 executions without the window. Names tf_mhc_branch as the one kernel
that cost the gap, the bit-identical fix that closed 3.1x of it, and that the
remaining 33-of-83 wants a reduction order change that needs an FP64 reference.
---
 docs/recipes/sensenova-loop-9b-metal.md | 23 ++++++++++++++---------
 zig/src/families/qwen27/mhc.zig         |  2 +-
 2 files changed, 15 insertions(+), 10 deletions(-)

diff --git a/docs/recipes/sensenova-loop-9b-metal.md b/docs/recipes/sensenova-loop-9b-metal.md
index 46927f8e0..bac04ffae 100644
--- a/docs/recipes/sensenova-loop-9b-metal.md
+++ b/docs/recipes/sensenova-loop-9b-metal.md
@@ -61,18 +61,23 @@ Through the served API with `tools/bench_openai.py`, 64 tokens, two repetitions,
 
 | metric | looped | plain 32-layer control |
 | --- | --- | --- |
-| decode | **10.77 tok/s** | **123.7 tok/s** |
+| decode | **33.19 tok/s** | **123.7 tok/s** |
 | prefill | 150.2 tok/s | 1457.8 tok/s |
-| TTFT | 0.093 s | 0.010 s |
-| inter-token latency | 92.8 ms | 8.09 ms |
+| TTFT | 0.032 s | 0.010 s |
 
 The plain control is the same binary and the same weights with `loop_settings.counts=[1]` and no `mhc`, so it
-isolates the loop and the mHC. 56 executions from 32 weight sets should cost about 1.75x a plain pass, which would
-be roughly 70 tok/s; the measured 10.77 is a further 6.5x. Removing only the mHC window (keeping 56 executions)
-recovers **82.0 tok/s**, so the cost is in the two-stream boundary, not the loop. Committing every 4 layers instead
-of 4 executions, and widening the router's threadgroups, changed nothing measurable. The open suspects are the
-8192-wide stream buffer being touched by two extra kernels per window layer, and 20-row dispatches over
-prompt-sized buffers. This is the next thing to fix, and these numbers should be re-taken after it.
+isolates the loop and the mHC. Keeping 56 executions but removing only the window gives **83.1 tok/s**, so the
+loop's own cost is as expected — and the window was the whole gap.
+
+The window was one kernel: `tf_mhc_branch`, the router. Skipping each mHC kernel in turn moved decode only for the
+router (11.3 -> 80.4 tok/s); `merge`, `enter` and `leave` were free. It dispatched **32 threads a row** and computed
+its eight outputs one after another, so each lane walked 128 elements per output per stream in a single simdgroup.
+An output a simdgroup — the same 32-lane order, run side by side — is **bit-identical** and takes decode from
+**11.3 to 35.3 tok/s** (33.19 through the served API). A wider 256-thread tree reduction reaches 42.4 but changes
+the fp32 reduction order and costs parity against the reference, so it is not landed.
+
+Remaining: 33 against the 83 no-window figure. The router is one threadgroup at decode (one row), so it cannot fill
+the GPU; splitting its work differently would change the reduction order and needs an FP64 reference first.
 
 ## Fidelity
 
diff --git a/zig/src/families/qwen27/mhc.zig b/zig/src/families/qwen27/mhc.zig
index 63f949f63..113767fef 100644
--- a/zig/src/families/qwen27/mhc.zig
+++ b/zig/src/families/qwen27/mhc.zig
@@ -62,7 +62,7 @@ pub fn enter(e: mtl.ComputeEncoder, k: Kernels, b: Buffers, p: Params, rows: u32
     e.dispatchThreads(mtl.Size.of(p.hidden, rows, 1), mtl.Size.of(threads(p.hidden), 1, 1));
 }
 
-/// Threads a width wants: a whole number of 32-lane groups, at most 256.
+/// Threads a width wants: whole 32-lane groups, at most 256; these kernels are elementwise so the width is free.
 fn threads(width: u32) u32 {
     return @min(@max(width + 31 & ~@as(u32, 31), 32), 256);
 }
